The "secretary problem" is as follows: you must interview secretaries in a random order and you know there are N secretaries. You do not know anything about the distribution of possible secretaries but for any two secretaries you can choose which you like better. After each interview you must either hire the secretary on the spot or reject them. If you reject them you cannot call them back. What is the optimal strategy to have the best chance of finding the best secretary?

The solution is this: reject the first 1/e secretaries then hire the next secretary which is better than all previous secretaries. This also gives a 1/e probability of finding the best secretary.

See: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZWib5olGbQ0

We can frame optimizatiton as two problems: first, break the search space into "basins" where anywhere in the same basin eventually leads to the same local optimum according to a local seaarch algorithm. Find the best basin, then given you are in the best basin, find the local optimum.

